{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The proposed abstraction claims all four foundations are representable as monotone edge addition on non-committed-root nodes, but if SRC_008 (not explicitly detailed here but referenced) preserves validity through a node-relabeling or edge-contraction mechanism rather than edge addition, the abstraction is refuted because contraction is not representable as monotone edge addition.","target":"5540dfc04111cfb01a6a53aed70345c55c93956ddde058306dc46f81b70bb456"},{"attack":false,"case":"The contradiction is substantively valid: a single monotone sweep over threshold τ produces one labeling, while SRC_011's operator on a finite lattice may permit multiple grounded labelings (not all matching the thresholded result), so the two can genuinely disagree. The refutation condition (unique least fixed point always matching) is a strong constraint that is not guaranteed for arbitrary weighted attack graphs.","target":"5f55db14e6d5424f2f91da96646832d32d15c899b53bbdfd77c438c840cb8e1f"},{"attack":true,"case":"The claim that defense chains and auxiliary support chains yield identical grounded labelings when bounds are equal conflates two different structural roles: defense chains block attacks on a node, while support chains provide independent warrant for a node. A graph where node A is attacked by B and defended by C (depth 1) can ground A, while a support chain of length 1 from D to A may not suffice to ground A if D itself is ungrounded, producing disagreement despite equal bounds.","counterexample":[{"graph":"A <- B (attack); C -> B (defense, depth 1); D -> A (support, length 1)","result":"Defense chain grounds A (B blocked by grounded C), support chain does not ground A (D ungrounded), disagreeing for equal bound 1"}],"target":"e322349b06e573ab757247acf7b3afa79618ac128cdc5269a093be7e8f98f684"},{"attack":false,"case":"The inheritance claim is coherent: SRC_007 requires re-examination through a fresh edge-construction pass, and inheriting SRC_009's anchor library as the canonical source means the pass should reproduce the same materializations. The refutation condition (fresh pass commits a new warrant changing the attack graph on a committed root despite matching the anchor library) would indeed invalidate the inheritance, but no specific structural reason is given why this must occur, so the claim stands as a plausible substantive relation.","target":"ff66558dc1b6319a9b5758b872653e63aa3b4280202148471ab8c5e201006f16"}]}